[Event "Exercise 130"]
[Site "Volgograd"]
[Date "1985.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Mikhail Tal"]
[Black "Eduardas Rozentalis"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "2rr2k1/pbqnbppp/1p3n2/2pp4/3P4/1PNBPN2/PB2QPPP/2RR2K1 w - - 0 14"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Source "book.pdf, p. 103"]

14. Bf5 $1
{ Activating the bishop before Black plays ...Ne4. It is better on f5 than on b5 for a few reasons, the main one being that it pins the d7- knight. Moreover, threatening the bishop with ...g7-g6 slightly weakens the black king – and if it happens, the bishop can stay on its newfound diagonal by retreating to h3. }
14... g6 15. Bh3 Ra8
{ Moving out of the pin before Ne5 can be played. White’s 14th move has paid off. }
( 15... Ne4 { runs into: } 16. Nxe4 $1
{ White could maintain some advantage by quieter means, but the concrete approach works well. }
16... dxe4 17. Nd2 $1 ( 17. Ne5 $2 Nxe5 $1 18. Bxc8 Nd3 $10 ) 17... Bf6 18. Ba3
{ White wins a pawn. } ) 16. Ne5 ( 16. Ba1 $5
{ was interesting, to use the diagonal with Qb2. } ) 16... Nxe5 17. dxe5 Ne4
18. Nxe4 dxe4 19. e6 { ↑ } *


[Event "Exercise 131"]
[Site "Kolkata"]
[Date "1986.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Viswanathan Anand"]
[Black "Vlastimil Jansa"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "4rrk1/1pqnbppp/pNbpp3/P7/4P3/4B1P1/1PP2PBP/R2QR1K1 b - - 0 15"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Source "book.pdf, p. 104"]

15... f5 $1
{ Otherwise Black’s rook would look – and be! – stupid. Still, White’s position is to be preferred slightly since Black will soon have three pawn islands against two. (And with several minor pieces exchanged, the two centre pawns don’t cramp White.) }
( 15... Nxb6 16. Bxb6 Qd7 17. c4 ) 16. Nxd7 Qxd7 17. Qd3 fxe4 18. Bxe4 Bxe4 19.
Qxe4 d5 { = } *


